```c
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {

  // Create the FIFO if it doesn't exist
  mkfifo("my_fifo", 0666);

  int fd = open("my_fifo", O_WRONLY);

  // Writer process
  if(fork() == 0) {
    char *str = "Hello World!";
    write(fd, str, strlen(str));
    close(fd);
  }

  // Reader process  
  else {
    fd = open("my_fifo", O_RDONLY);
    char buf[80];
    read(fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    printf("Received: %s\n", buf); 
    close(fd);
  }

  // Delete the FIFO
  unlink("my_fifo");

  return 0;
}
```

Here we create a named pipe "my_fifo", then fork() to create a separate writer and reader process. The writer writes a message to the FIFO which the reader then reads and prints.

Some potential issues:

- Race conditions if multiple processes try to open the FIFO simultaneously
- Blocking if a reader/writer is not yet available
- Managing consistency of data between multiple writer/reader processes

So care needs to be taken with synchronization and coordination between processes. But this shows the basic mechanism of transferring data.

